.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0
.TH WS-DIRECTION 1 2026-11-01 "ws-direction 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ws\-direction \- open and close the workspace overview of the ws-direction picom plugin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ws\-direction overview open
.RB [ \-\-all " | " \-\-output
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-wait " [" \-\-buttons ]]
.br
.B ws\-direction overview close
.br
.B ws\-direction overview toggle
.br
.B ws\-direction \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ws\-direction
is the command a window manager config calls to control the ws-direction plugin, which
runs inside
.BR picom (1).
The command sends the plugin one event per command, and the plugin answers it.
The plugin starts the animations; the styles in picom's config say what they look like.
Commands that come fast, like two toggles, are each done, in order.
.PP
When picom does not run with the plugin, the command says so and fails.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B overview open
Unfold the monitor with the focus (the one that shows
.BR \%_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP )
into an overview of its workspaces, or the monitors that
.B \-\-all
or
.B \-\-output
name.
The plugin starts the animation trigger
.B ws\-unfold
on every window of that monitor's workspaces, and again on every workspace switch on
that monitor while the overview is open.
The monitors it doesn't name don't take part.
A monitor that is open already stays as it is.
.IP
While a monitor is unfolded, the plugin takes its clicks with a window of its own over
the monitor, so they don't land on windows that are drawn somewhere else.
The other monitors work as usual.
A click of the left button on a window in the overview picks it: on the release, the
plugin asks the window manager to show the window and focus it, as a pager does, and
closes the overview once the window's workspace shows.
A click where no window is drawn does nothing, and so do the other buttons and the
wheel, unless a command waits for them
.RB ( \-\-buttons ).
.TP
.B overview close
Fold every unfolded monitor.
The plugin starts the trigger
.B ws\-fold
on their windows.
.TP
.B overview toggle
Close when the monitor with the focus is unfolded, else open.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Only after
.BR "overview open" .
Unfold every monitor.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " NAME"
Only after
.BR "overview open" .
Unfold the monitor with RandR name
.IR NAME ,
as
.B xrandr \-\-listmonitors
shows it.
It fails when no monitor of that name has workspaces of its own.
.TP
.B \-\-wait
Only after
.BR "overview open" .
Don't return at once: wait until a click on a window closes the overview, and exit with
status 0, or until an
.B overview close
or
.B overview toggle
command comes, whatever it does, and exit with status 1.
A window manager config that opens the overview in a mode of its own can leave that mode
after a click this way; its keys that close the overview leave the mode themselves.
.TP
.B \-\-buttons
Only with
.BR \-\-wait .
Also return when another button is pressed while a monitor is unfolded, with exit status
10 plus the button's number: 12 for the middle button, 13 for the right one, 14 and 15
for the wheel up and down, 16 and 17 for left and right.
The overview stays open; the window manager config says what each button does, and runs
the command again to wait for the next one.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.B ws\-direction
and the version, and exit.
.PP
There is no
.BR \-\-help :
any other arguments print the usage to standard error, with exit status 2.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The plugin did the command, or the version was printed.
With
.BR \-\-wait ,
a click on a window closed the overview.
.TP
.B 1
The command could not connect to the X server, picom does not run with the plugin, the
plugin speaks another version of the protocol, or it could not do the command; a message
says which.
With
.BR \-\-wait ,
also when a close or toggle command came, or picom stopped.
.TP
.B 2
The arguments were wrong.
.TP
.B 12 \(en 19
With
.BR "\-\-wait \-\-buttons" ,
button 2 to 9 was pressed while a monitor was unfolded.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X display to talk to.
It must be the display picom runs on.
.TP
.B WS_DIRECTION_LOG
Set it in picom's environment, not the command's, to a file, and the plugin logs every
decision there, in picom's log format.
The first line is
.B ws\-direction
and the version; if the plugin could not start, the line after it says why.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/picom/plugins/ws\-direction.so
The plugin, where
.B make install
puts it; picom finds it there by its name.
A package puts it in picom's plugin directory, which is
.I \%picom/plugins
under the library directory picom was installed to, for example
.IR \%/usr/lib/picom/plugins/ .
The picom fork finds it there by its name; picom releases need its full path in
.BR plugins .
.TP
.I ~/.config/picom/include/ws\-direction/
The styles, where
.B make install
puts them.
A package puts them in
.IR \%/etc/xdg/picom/include/ws\-direction/ .
picom looks in the directory of
.I picom.conf
first, so a copy of a style in
.I ws\-direction/
there wins over both.
.SH THE PLUGIN
The plugin needs plugin API 0.9 of the picom fork.
Load it in
.I picom.conf
and include a style for workspace switches:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
plugins = "ws\-direction.so";
rules = ({
  match = "!override_redirect && window_type = \(aqnormal\(aq";
  @include "ws\-direction/slide.conf"
});
.fi
.RE
.PP
The overview needs a style with animations for
.B ws\-unfold
and
.BR ws\-fold ,
in a second rule with the same match.
It draws hidden workspaces from the images picom keeps of them; add
.B keep\-image\-when\-hidden = true;
to that rule.
A workspace picom has not shown since it started has no images, and shows no windows.
The plugin declares its triggers
.BR ws\-pass ,
.B ws\-unfold
and
.BR ws\-fold ,
so the config doesn't list them in
.BR animation\-triggers .
.PP
The command and the plugin talk through the selection
.BR \%_WS_DIRECTION_S \fIscreen\fR,
which the plugin owns, and client messages, which may change between versions.
Use the command.
.SH ANIMATION VARIABLES
picom asks the plugin for these when an animation whose script reads one starts.
The README has the details.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-direction
1 if the new workspace sorts after the old one, \-1 if before, 0 if the animation is not
part of a workspace switch on the window's monitor: the window manager didn't show or
hide the window to make the switch, or the plugin didn't start the trigger for it.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-distance
How many of the monitor's workspaces the switch moved; 0 when the direction is 0.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-monitor\-x
The left edge of the monitor that shows the window's workspace, also while it is hidden;
for a window that closes, of the whole screen.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-monitor\-y
The top edge of that monitor.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-monitor\-width
The width of that monitor.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-monitor\-height
The height of that monitor.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-faces
How many workspaces that monitor has.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-face
How many of the monitor's workspaces the window's workspace is away from the one shown
before the switch (outside a switch, the one shown now): positive after it, negative
before.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-selected
1 if that monitor shows the current workspace, else 0.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-placed
0 if the window is on one workspace but its centre is not on that workspace's monitor,
else 1.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-index
Where the window's workspace is among the monitor's workspaces, counted from 0 in their
order.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-shown
The index of the workspace the monitor shows now; \-1 while the plugin can't tell.
.TP
.B plugin\-ws\-direction\-unfolded
1 while the monitor is unfolded into an overview, else 0.
.SH TRIGGERS
The plugin starts these custom animation triggers on the windows of a monitor's
workspaces.
picom ignores a start on a window whose rule has no animation for the trigger, or that
it has no image of.
.TP
.B ws\-pass
On every workspace switch, on every window of the monitor of the switch, in the pass in
which the plugin sees it; not on a window that moved to the new workspace with the
switch.
.TP
.B ws\-unfold
When the overview opens on a monitor, on every window of its workspaces and on the
windows on all desktops there (with the index of the shown workspace); again, instead of
.BR ws\-pass ,
on every switch on that monitor while it is open; and again when its workspaces or
windows change while it is open.
.TP
.B ws\-fold
When the overview closes, on every window of the monitors that were open.
After a click on a window, the window's monitor folds together with the switch to the
window's workspace, or at once if that workspace shows already.
.SH EXAMPLES
An
.BR i3 (1)
overview that $mod+Tab opens, the arrow keys move through, and Return, Escape, $mod+Tab
or a click on a window close:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
set $overview exec \-\-no\-startup\-id ws\-direction overview
bindsym $mod+Tab mode "overview"; $overview open \-\-wait && i3\-msg \-q mode default
mode "overview" {
    bindsym Left workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym Up workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym Right workspace next_on_output
    bindsym Down workspace next_on_output
    bindsym Return mode "default"; $overview close
    bindsym Escape mode "default"; $overview close
    bindsym $mod+Tab mode "default"; $overview close
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B open \-\-wait
returns 0 once a click on a window closed the overview, and then
.B i3\-msg
takes i3 out of the overview mode.
.PP
To give the other buttons something to do, run the command again after each one, for
example from a script that the binding runs:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
while :; do
    ws\-direction overview open \-\-wait \-\-buttons
    case $? in
    14) i3\-msg \-q workspace prev_on_output ;;
    15) i3\-msg \-q workspace next_on_output ;;
    0) i3\-msg \-q mode default; exit ;;
    *) exit ;;
    esac
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
An overview that shows only while the key is held:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bindsym $mod+Tab $overview open
bindsym \-\-release $mod+Tab $overview close
.fi
.RE
.PP
The window manager must find
.B ws\-direction
on its PATH.
.B make install
puts it in
.IR ~/.local/bin ,
which is often not on the PATH of a window manager that a display manager starts; then
give the full path.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR picom (1),
.BR i3 (1)
.PP
The README of this release has the details of the variables and the triggers, the
styles, how to write one, and the limits:
.RS 4
\%https://github.com/JornC/picom\-ws\-direction\-plugin/blob/v0.2.0/README.md
.RE
